#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ctlhq-commit: commit named paths with a four-section message file after a green gate, then verify hash, signature, and clean tree.

Usage:
  ctlhq-commit [--gate NAME] [--subject TEXT] [--why TEXT] [--changes TEXT]
                [--test-plan TEXT] [--risks TEXT] [--message-file FILE]
                [--co-author LINE] [--allow-dirty] -- <path> [path...]

Agents commit only through this tool, which adds the co-author trailer.
The trailer names the model that made the commit, and only the agent knows its model.

Steps, in order:
  1. Needs at least one path after "--" (exit 2). Every path is added by name: ".", "-A",
     "--all", and anything that starts with "-" are refused (exit 2).
  2. With --gate NAME, reads .local/gate/NAME.exit (written by ctlhq-run-gate). A missing file or
     anything but 0 refuses (exit 3) and prints the exit it found and the .meta line.
     It also reads .local/gate/NAME.tree, which holds the same run's exit and the tree ID
     it tested. A missing file, an "error" line (its reason is printed), an exit other
     than 0, or no tree line refuses (exit 3) with what, why:, and fix: lines; the fix is
     to rerun the gate. Step 6 compares the tree ID with this tree. Without --gate the
     tool prints "gate: not checked".
  3. Builds the message: --subject on the first line, a blank line, then the four sections
     "Why:", "Changes:", "Test Plan:", "Risks:", each heading alone on its own line with
     the flag's text below it. All five flags are required, or --message-file FILE is
     taken verbatim instead (it excludes the five flags). The message is written to
     .local/commit/<UTC timestamp>.txt at the top of the work tree.
  4. Co-author trailer. A message that already has a Co-Authored-By line keeps it, and
     nothing is added. Otherwise one line is appended, taken from the first of:
       a. --co-author "Co-Authored-By: Name <email>"
       b. the environment variable CTLHQ_CO_AUTHOR
     The line must have the shape "Co-Authored-By: Name <email>" on one line. There is
     no default line: with no trailer in the message and no source, or a bad line, the
     tool refuses (exit 5) with what, why:, and fix: lines. The refusal names the running
     agent when it can tell (AI_AGENT starting with claude-code means Claude Code;
     CODEX_THREAD_ID means Codex). The tool prints "co-author: <line> (from <source>)".
  5. Every git call that takes the named paths reads them as plain names, never as
     patterns (`git --literal-pathspecs`), so "src/*.py", ":/" or ":!a.txt" match only a
     file with exactly that name. A path neither on disk nor tracked refuses (exit 1)
     before anything is staged. Refuses (exit 3, with what, why:, and fix: lines) when the
     index already holds a staged change (`git diff --cached --ignore-submodules=none`
     against HEAD, so a submodule's staged commit counts whatever the ignore settings say)
     to a path that is not named and not inside a named folder, because `git commit` takes
     the whole index. Then it keeps a copy of the index and stages each named path by
     name: a path on disk with `git add -A -- <path>`, so deletions inside a named folder
     are staged too; a path gone from disk but still tracked (or already staged as deleted) with
     `git rm -r --cached -- <path>`. Unnamed paths are never staged. Any refusal or
     failure from here until the commit exists puts the index back byte for byte from
     that copy (written to index.lock, then renamed, as git does), so partly staged files
     and staged files from before the first commit stay as they were. So does an
     interrupt (Ctrl-C or SIGTERM) or an unexpected error, as long as HEAD has not moved;
     the run then ends as the interrupt or error would. The tool's `git add`, `git rm`,
     `git write-tree` and `git commit` run with splitIndex.sharedIndexExpire=never, so a
     split index never loses the shared file that copy points to. File names that are not
     UTF-8 pass through unchanged.
  6. With --gate NAME, just before the commit, runs `ctlhq-run-gate --tree` and compares
     its tree ID with the one in .local/gate/NAME.tree. The tree ID covers the whole work
     tree on disk, whatever paths are named, and leaves out the HEAD commit, so one gate
     run covers several commits of the files it tested. A difference refuses (exit 3)
     with what, why:, and fix: lines and commits nothing.
     Then it takes the index tree (`git write-tree`) and lists every path whose entry
     differs between HEAD's tree (the empty tree before the first commit) and the index
     tree, submodules included. With or without --gate, it refuses (exit 3, naming the
     paths) when:
       a. a listed path is not a named path and not inside a named folder (paths are
          compared from the top of the work tree), or
       b. a listed path has a different entry (mode and object ID, or absence) in the
          reference tree: the gate's tree with --gate, otherwise the tree ID of the files
          on disk from `ctlhq-run-gate --tree`, taken just then. When that tree ID cannot
          be taken, the run refuses too.
     Check b catches a staged copy that differs from the file on disk, such as one marked
     assume-unchanged or skip-worktree.
     Then it runs `git commit -F <file>` (no -m, no --no-verify, no --amend) and verifies:
     the commit's tree equals the index tree taken just before (a pre-commit hook that
     changed the index breaks this; the changed paths are printed and the run exits 1),
     every named path is committed (`git status --porcelain -- <paths>` empty; a hook
     that changed the files can break this), and the tree is clean, or with --allow-dirty
     the other dirty paths are printed and accepted. --allow-dirty never accepts the first
     two. In those two cases the fix: line says the commit exists and how to undo it. When
     HEAD's parent is not the commit HEAD named before `git commit` ran (a hook made
     another commit), it names that earlier commit and leaves out `git reset --soft HEAD~1`.
     Prints the hash, the subject, the signature state from `git log --show-signature -1`
     (signed, unsigned, or unknown with git's %G? code), the tree state, and last
     "committed <hash>".
  7. Writes the receipt .local/commits/<full hash>.json in the main work tree (the folder
     that holds the git common dir, so a receipt outlives a linked worktree) and prints
     "receipt: <path>". It is written whenever the commit exists, also when step 6 then
     exits 1. Keys: hash, subject, branch, committed_at (UTC), paths, message_file,
     co_author, signature, tree ("clean", "dirty", "named paths not committed", or
     "changed by a hook"), dirty_paths, hook_changed (the paths a hook changed), gate
     (null without --gate, else name, exit, and meta: the .meta line), and exit (this
     run's exit code). .local/ is ignored by git, so the receipt needs no
     receipt commit: PLAN.md cites the hash in the next real commit. When the receipt
     cannot be written, stderr says "receipt: not written (<error>)" and the run goes on;
     the receipt never changes the exit code.

Exit codes: 0 success; 1 git refused (no work tree, a named path missing, add or commit
failed), a hook changed the commit, a named path is not committed afterwards, or the
tree is dirty afterwards without --allow-dirty (in the last three cases the commit exists
and its hash is printed); 2 usage; 3 gate not green, it ran on a different tree, the
index would commit content the gate did not test or that differs from the files on
disk, or the index holds staged changes outside the named paths; 5 no valid co-author
line (4 is retired). An interrupt ends the run as Python does: 130 for Ctrl-C, 143 for
SIGTERM.

.local/ must be ignored by git, or the message file itself dirties the tree.

Operator rule (home/AGENTS.md, Working method): "Commit only through the sequence: gate exit line read directly (never through tail or grep), git add by name, git commit -F from a message file, signature and clean tree verified, hash recorded in PLAN.md. Only then say 'committed <hash>'." This tool runs that sequence; recording the hash in PLAN.md stays with you.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import datetime
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

NAME_RE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$")
CO_AUTHOR_RE = re.compile(r"^Co-Authored-By: [^<>\n]*[^<>\s] <[^<>\s@]+@[^<>\s@]+>$")
HAS_CO_AUTHOR_RE = re.compile(r"(?im)^co-authored-by:")
TRAILER_LINE_RE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9-]*: \S")
CO_AUTHOR_SHAPE = 'Co-Authored-By: Name <email>'
# Without it, a git call that writes a split index may delete the shared index file the saved index copy points to.
KEEP_SHARED = ["-c", "splitIndex.sharedIndexExpire=never"]
MARKS_FIX = ("stage the files as they are on disk (a file marked assume-unchanged or skip-worktree keeps its old staged "
             "copy; `git update-index --no-assume-unchanged --no-skip-worktree <path>` clears both marks), ")
RUN_GATE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "ctlhq-run-gate"
USAGE = (
    "usage: ctlhq-commit [--gate NAME] [--subject TEXT] [--why TEXT] [--changes TEXT]\n"
    "                     [--test-plan TEXT] [--risks TEXT] [--message-file FILE]\n"
    "                     [--co-author LINE] [--allow-dirty] -- <path> [path...]"
)
SIG_CODES = {
    "G": "good signature",
    "B": "bad signature",
    "U": "good signature, unknown validity",
    "X": "good signature, expired",
    "Y": "good signature, expired key",
    "R": "good signature, revoked key",
    "E": "signature cannot be checked (missing key or no allowed signers file)",
    "N": "no signature",
}


def usage(msg: str) -> int:
    print(f"ctlhq-commit: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    print(USAGE, file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


class CoAuthorError(Exception):
    def __init__(self, what: str, why: str, fix: str) -> None:
        super().__init__(what)
        self.what, self.why, self.fix = what, why, fix


CO_AUTHOR_WHY = "Agent commits carry a Co-Authored-By trailer, and ctlhq-commit adds it."


def agent_name() -> str:
    agent = os.environ.get("AI_AGENT", "")
    if agent.startswith("claude-code"):
        return "Claude Code"
    if os.environ.get("CODEX_THREAD_ID"):
        return "Codex"
    return agent


def co_author_line(flag: str | None) -> tuple[str, str]:
    if flag is not None:
        line, source = flag, "--co-author"
    elif os.environ.get("CTLHQ_CO_AUTHOR"):
        line, source = os.environ["CTLHQ_CO_AUTHOR"], "CTLHQ_CO_AUTHOR"
    else:
        agent = agent_name()
        raise CoAuthorError(
            f"no Co-Authored-By line: the message has none, and there is no --co-author and no CTLHQ_CO_AUTHOR"
            + (f" (agent: {agent})" if agent else ""),
            CO_AUTHOR_WHY + " The line names the model that made the commit, and only the agent knows its model.",
            "add the Co-Authored-By line your agent gives for its current model to the message, or pass --co-author "
            '"Co-Authored-By: Name <email>".')
    if not CO_AUTHOR_RE.match(line):
        raise CoAuthorError(f"the co-author line from {source} is {line!r}, not the shape {CO_AUTHOR_SHAPE!r}",
                            "git and GitHub read the trailer only in that exact one-line shape.",
                            f'fix {source} to "Co-Authored-By: Name <email>", for example "Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>".')
    return line, source


def add_trailer(message: str, line: str) -> str:
    body = message.rstrip("\n")
    last = body.split("\n\n")[-1].split("\n")
    if "\n\n" in body and all(TRAILER_LINE_RE.match(l) for l in last):
        return f"{body}\n{line}\n"
    return f"{body}\n\n{line}\n"


def git(args: list[str]) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    # surrogateescape keeps file names that are not UTF-8 intact on the way back to git.
    return subprocess.run(["git"] + args, capture_output=True, encoding="utf-8", errors="surrogateescape")


def lit(args: list[str]) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    """git with every pathspec read as a plain path, so a named 'src/*.py' or ':/' matches only that name."""
    return git(["--literal-pathspecs"] + args)


def gate_tree(gate: str, root: Path) -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:
    """The tree ID the gate's green run tested, or None and the reason there is none."""
    path = root / ".local" / "gate" / f"{gate}.tree"
    if not path.is_file():
        return None, f"no file .local/gate/{gate}.tree, so nothing shows which tree the gate tested"
    record = dict(l.split(" ", 1) for l in path.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").splitlines() if " " in l)
    if "error" in record:
        return None, f"the gate could not record its tree: {record['error']}"
    if "tree" not in record:
        return None, f".local/gate/{gate}.tree has no tree line, so it was written by an older ctlhq-run-gate"
    if record.get("exit") != "0":
        return None, f".local/gate/{gate}.tree says exit {record.get('exit', '(none)')}, so .local/gate/{gate}.exit comes from another run"
    return record["tree"], None


def tree_now() -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:
    r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(RUN_GATE), "--tree"], capture_output=True, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None, f"`ctlhq-run-gate --tree` failed (exit {r.returncode}): {r.stderr.strip()}"
    return r.stdout.strip(), None


def refuse_gate(gate: str, reason: str, meta_line: str, listed: list[str] | None = None,
                why: str = "a change after the gate started was never checked.", fix: str = "") -> int:
    print(f"ctlhq-commit: gate {gate}: exit 0, but {reason}; refusing to commit", file=sys.stderr)
    for n in listed or []:
        print(f"  {n}", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"why: a gate result proves only the tree it tested, and {why}", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"fix: {fix}rerun `{rerun_line(gate, meta_line)}`, then run ctlhq-commit again.", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"gate {gate} meta: {meta_line}", file=sys.stderr)
    return 3


def base_tree() -> str:
    """HEAD's tree, or the empty tree before the first commit."""
    head = git(["rev-parse", "--verify", "-q", "HEAD^{tree}"])
    return head.stdout.strip() if head.returncode == 0 else git(["hash-object", "-t", "tree", "/dev/null"]).stdout.strip()


def top_relative(p: str, root: Path, prefix: str) -> str:
    if os.path.isabs(p):
        parent, name = os.path.split(os.path.normpath(p))
        return os.path.normpath(os.path.join(os.path.relpath(os.path.realpath(parent), root.resolve()), name))
    return os.path.normpath(os.path.join(prefix, p))


def covered(path: str, named: list[str]) -> bool:
    """Whether a top-relative path is a named path or inside a named folder."""
    return any(n == "." or path == n or path.startswith(n + "/") for n in named)


def unnamed_staged(named: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Paths staged against HEAD (or the empty tree before the first commit) that no named path covers."""
    r = git(["diff", "--cached", "--name-only", "-z", "--no-renames", "--no-relative", "--ignore-submodules=none", base_tree()])
    if r.returncode != 0:
        raise OSError(f"git diff --cached failed (exit {r.returncode}): {r.stderr.strip()}")
    return [s for s in r.stdout.split("\0") if s and not covered(s, named)]


def refuse_unnamed(what: str, listed: list[str]) -> int:
    print(f"ctlhq-commit: {what}; refusing to commit:", file=sys.stderr)
    for n in listed:
        print(f"  {n}", file=sys.stderr)
    print("why: git commit takes the whole index, so these changes would go in unnamed, and no gate checked the staged copy.", file=sys.stderr)
    print("fix: name them after --, or unstage them with `git reset -q -- <path>`, then run ctlhq-commit again.", file=sys.stderr)
    return 3


def tree_diff(a: str, b: str, status: bool = False) -> list[str]:
    """Paths whose entry (mode and object ID, or presence) differs between trees a and b."""
    r = git(["diff-tree", "-r", "-z", "--no-renames", "--ignore-submodules=none",
             "--name-status" if status else "--name-only", a, b])
    if r.returncode != 0:
        raise OSError(f"git diff-tree {a[:12]} {b[:12]} failed (exit {r.returncode}): {r.stderr.strip()}")
    fields = [f for f in r.stdout.split("\0") if f]
    return [f"{fields[i]} {fields[i + 1]}" for i in range(0, len(fields), 2)] if status else fields


class Index:
    """The user's index as it was before staging, to put back byte for byte when the run refuses."""

    def __init__(self) -> None:
        self.path = Path(git(["rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-path", "index"]).stdout.strip())
        self.saved = self.path.read_bytes() if self.path.is_file() else None

    def restore(self) -> None:
        if (self.path.read_bytes() if self.path.is_file() else None) == self.saved:
            return
        lock = self.path.with_name(self.path.name + ".lock")
        try:
            fd = os.open(lock, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, 0o666)
        except OSError as e:
            print(f"ctlhq-commit: the index still holds the named paths staged; restoring it failed: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
            return
        try:
            with os.fdopen(fd, "wb") as f:
                f.write(self.saved or b"")
            if self.saved is None:
                self.path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
                lock.unlink()
            else:
                os.replace(lock, self.path)
        except OSError as e:
            lock.unlink(missing_ok=True)
            print(f"ctlhq-commit: the index still holds the named paths staged; restoring it failed: {e}", file=sys.stderr)


def rerun_line(gate: str, meta_line: str) -> str:
    fields = meta_line.split("\t")
    command = fields[3] if len(fields) >= 4 else "<check command>"
    name = "" if gate == "gate" else f"--name {shlex.quote(gate)} "
    return f"ctlhq-run-gate {name}-- {command}"


def signature_state() -> str:
    cat = git(["cat-file", "commit", "HEAD"]).stdout
    headers = cat.split("\n\n", 1)[0]
    has_sig = any(line.startswith("gpgsig") for line in headers.split("\n"))
    r = git(["log", "--show-signature", "-1", "--format=%G?"])
    lines = [l.strip() for l in (r.stdout or "").split("\n") if l.strip()]
    code = lines[-1] if lines and lines[-1] in SIG_CODES else ""
    if code in ("G", "U"):
        return "signed"
    if not has_sig and code in ("N", ""):
        return "unsigned"
    if has_sig:
        return f"unknown (signature present, git %G? = {code or '?'}: {SIG_CODES.get(code, 'no verdict')})"
    return f"unknown (no gpgsig header, git %G? = {code})"


def committed_at() -> str | None:
    ct = git(["log", "-1", "--format=%ct"]).stdout.strip()
    if not ct.isdigit():
        return None
    return datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp(int(ct), datetime.timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")


def write_receipt(root: Path, receipt: dict) -> str:
    r = git(["rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir"])
    common = Path(r.stdout.strip()) if r.returncode == 0 and r.stdout.strip() else None
    base = common.parent if common is not None and common.name == ".git" else root
    folder = base / ".local" / "commits"
    folder.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    path = folder / f"{receipt['hash']}.json"
    path.write_text(json.dumps(receipt, indent=2) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
    try:
        return path.relative_to(root).as_posix()
    except ValueError:
        return str(path)


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, lambda signum, frame: sys.exit(128 + signum))
    sys.stdout.reconfigure(errors="backslashreplace")
    gate: str | None = None
    subject = why = changes = test_plan = risks = None
    message_file: str | None = None
    co_author: str | None = None
    allow_dirty = False
    paths: list[str] | None = None
    valued = {"--gate", "--subject", "--why", "--changes", "--test-plan", "--risks", "--message-file", "--co-author"}
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        a = argv[i]
        if a == "--":
            paths = argv[i + 1:]
            break
        if a in ("-h", "--help"):
            print(__doc__.strip())
            return 0
        if a in valued:
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                return usage(f"{a} needs a value")
            val = argv[i + 1]
            if a == "--gate":
                if not NAME_RE.match(val):
                    return usage(f"--gate {val!r}: use letters, digits, '.', '-', '_' only")
                gate = val
            elif a == "--subject":
                subject = val
            elif a == "--why":
                why = val
            elif a == "--changes":
                changes = val
            elif a == "--test-plan":
                test_plan = val
            elif a == "--risks":
                risks = val
            elif a == "--co-author":
                co_author = val
            else:
                message_file = val
            i += 2
            continue
        if a == "--allow-dirty":
            allow_dirty = True
            i += 1
            continue
        return usage(f"unknown option {a!r} (paths go after --)")

    # 1. paths by name
    if paths is None:
        return usage("missing -- before the paths")
    if not paths:
        return usage("no paths after --; git add is by name, never -A or .")
    for p in paths:
        if p in (".", "..", "-A", "--all", "*") or p.startswith("-"):
            return usage(f"refusing path {p!r}; git add is by name, never -A or .")

    # message inputs
    flags = [("--subject", subject), ("--why", why), ("--changes", changes), ("--test-plan", test_plan), ("--risks", risks)]
    if message_file is not None:
        given = [f for f, v in flags if v is not None]
        if given:
            return usage(f"--message-file excludes {', '.join(given)}")
        try:
            message = Path(message_file).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        except OSError as e:
            return usage(f"cannot read --message-file {message_file}: {e.strerror or e}")
        if not message.strip():
            return usage(f"--message-file {message_file} is empty")
    else:
        missing = [f for f, v in flags if v is None]
        if missing:
            return usage(f"missing {', '.join(missing)}; or pass --message-file FILE")
        empty = [f for f, v in flags if not (v or "").strip()]
        if empty:
            return usage(f"empty text for {', '.join(empty)}")
        assert subject is not None and why is not None and changes is not None and test_plan is not None and risks is not None
        if "\n" in subject.strip():
            return usage("--subject must be one line")
        sections = [("Why:", why), ("Changes:", changes), ("Test Plan:", test_plan), ("Risks:", risks)]
        message = subject.strip() + "\n\n" + "\n\n".join(f"{h}\n{t.strip()}" for h, t in sections) + "\n"

    # co-author trailer
    if HAS_CO_AUTHOR_RE.search(message):
        co_author_note = "co-author: kept the message's own Co-Authored-By line"
    else:
        try:
            line, source = co_author_line(co_author)
        except CoAuthorError as e:
            print(f"ctlhq-commit: {e.what}\nwhy: {e.why}\nfix: {e.fix}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 5
        message = add_trailer(message, line)
        co_author_note = f"co-author: {line} (from {source})"

    # repository
    r = git(["rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])
    if r.returncode != 0 or not r.stdout.strip():
        print(f"ctlhq-commit: not inside a git work tree: {Path.cwd()}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    root = Path(r.stdout.strip())

    # 2. gate
    gate_record = None
    if gate is None:
        print("gate: not checked (no --gate)")
    else:
        exit_path = root / ".local" / "gate" / f"{gate}.exit"
        meta_path = root / ".local" / "gate" / f"{gate}.meta"
        meta_line = meta_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").strip() if meta_path.is_file() else "(no .meta line)"
        if not exit_path.is_file():
            print(f"ctlhq-commit: gate {gate}: no file .local/gate/{gate}.exit; run `ctlhq-run-gate --name {gate} -- <command>` first", file=sys.stderr)
            return 3
        found = exit_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").strip()
        if found != "0":
            print(f"ctlhq-commit: gate {gate}: exit {found or '(empty)'}, not 0; refusing to commit", file=sys.stderr)
            print(f"gate {gate} meta: {meta_line}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 3
        tested, why_none = gate_tree(gate, root)
        if tested is None:
            return refuse_gate(gate, why_none, meta_line)
        gate_record = {"name": gate, "exit": 0, "meta": meta_line}

    # 3. message file
    commit_dir = root / ".local" / "commit"
    commit_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    stamp = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ")
    msg_path = commit_dir / f"{stamp}.txt"
    n = 1
    while msg_path.exists():
        n += 1
        msg_path = commit_dir / f"{stamp}-{n}.txt"
    msg_path.write_text(message if message.endswith("\n") else message + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
    try:
        rel_msg = msg_path.relative_to(root).as_posix()
    except ValueError:
        rel_msg = str(msg_path)
    print(f"message file: {rel_msg}")
    print(co_author_note)

    # 5. stage each named path, deletions included
    on_disk = [p for p in paths if os.path.lexists(p)]
    gone = [p for p in paths if p not in on_disk]
    missing = [p for p in gone if lit(["ls-files", "--error-unmatch", "--", p]).returncode != 0
               and not lit(["ls-tree", "-r", "--name-only", "HEAD", "--", p]).stdout.strip()]
    if missing:
        print(f"ctlhq-commit: {', '.join(missing)}: not on disk and not tracked by git, so there is nothing to stage", file=sys.stderr)
        print("fix: check the spelling, or drop the path from the list after --. Paths are names, never patterns.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    prefix = git(["rev-parse", "--show-prefix"]).stdout.strip()
    named = [top_relative(p, root, prefix) for p in paths]
    try:
        stray = unnamed_staged(named)
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"ctlhq-commit: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if stray:
        return refuse_unnamed("the index holds staged changes outside the named paths", stray)
    index = Index()
    head_before = git(["rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", "HEAD"]).stdout.strip() or None

    def undo(code: int) -> int:
        index.restore()
        return code

    try:
        if on_disk:
            r = lit(KEEP_SHARED + ["add", "-A", "--"] + on_disk)
            if r.returncode != 0:
                sys.stderr.write(r.stderr)
                print(f"ctlhq-commit: git add failed (exit {r.returncode})", file=sys.stderr)
                return undo(1)
        if gone:
            r = lit(KEEP_SHARED + ["rm", "-r", "-q", "--cached", "--ignore-unmatch", "--"] + gone)
            if r.returncode != 0:
                sys.stderr.write(r.stderr)
                print(f"ctlhq-commit: git rm --cached failed (exit {r.returncode})", file=sys.stderr)
                return undo(1)
        if gate is not None:
            now, why_none = tree_now()
            if now is None or now != tested:
                return undo(refuse_gate(gate, why_none or f"the files on disk differ from the ones it tested (tree {tested[:12]} then, {now[:12]} now)", meta_line))
        r = git(KEEP_SHARED + ["write-tree"])
        if r.returncode != 0:
            sys.stderr.write(r.stderr)
            print(f"ctlhq-commit: git write-tree failed (exit {r.returncode}), so the index cannot be committed", file=sys.stderr)
            return undo(1)
        staged_tree = r.stdout.strip()
        try:
            changed = tree_diff(base_tree(), staged_tree)
        except OSError as e:
            print(f"ctlhq-commit: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
            return undo(1)
        outside = [p for p in changed if not covered(p, named)]
        if outside:
            return undo(refuse_unnamed("the commit would change paths outside the named paths", outside))
        if gate is not None:
            reference = tested
        else:
            reference, why_none = tree_now()
            if reference is None:
                print(f"ctlhq-commit: cannot compare the commit with the files on disk: {why_none}; refusing to commit", file=sys.stderr)
                print("why: without that comparison, a staged copy that differs from the file on disk would go in unseen.", file=sys.stderr)
                print("fix: clear the cause above, then run ctlhq-commit again.", file=sys.stderr)
                return undo(3)
        try:
            unseen = set(tree_diff(staged_tree, reference))
        except OSError as e:
            print(f"ctlhq-commit: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
            return undo(1)
        differ = [p for p in changed if p in unseen]
        if differ and gate is not None:
            return undo(refuse_gate(
                gate, "the commit would hold content for these paths that differs from the tree it tested", meta_line, differ,
                why="git commit takes each path's staged copy, which differs here from the file the gate tested.",
                fix=MARKS_FIX + "or ",
            ))
        if differ:
            print("ctlhq-commit: the commit would hold content for these paths that differs from the files on disk; refusing to commit:", file=sys.stderr)
            for n in differ:
                print(f"  {n}", file=sys.stderr)
            print("why: git commit takes each path's staged copy, which differs here from the file on disk, so nobody has seen what would be committed.", file=sys.stderr)
            print(f"fix: {MARKS_FIX}then run ctlhq-commit again.", file=sys.stderr)
            return undo(3)
        if gate is not None:
            print(f"gate {gate}: exit 0 on this tree ({meta_line})")
        r = git(KEEP_SHARED + ["commit", "-F", str(msg_path)])
        if r.returncode != 0:
            sys.stderr.write(r.stdout)
            sys.stderr.write(r.stderr)
            print(f"ctlhq-commit: git commit failed (exit {r.returncode})", file=sys.stderr)
            return undo(1)
    except BaseException:
        # Once HEAD has moved, the index belongs to the new commit and must stay.
        if (git(["rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", "HEAD"]).stdout.strip() or None) == head_before:
            index.restore()
        raise

    full = git(["rev-parse", "HEAD"]).stdout.strip()
    subj = git(["log", "-1", "--format=%s"]).stdout.strip()
    dirty = [l for l in git(["status", "--porcelain"]).stdout.split("\n") if l.strip()]
    named_dirty = [l for l in lit(["status", "--porcelain", "--"] + paths).stdout.split("\n") if l.strip()]
    try:
        hooked = tree_diff(staged_tree, git(["rev-parse", "HEAD^{tree}"]).stdout.strip(), status=True)
    except OSError as e:
        hooked = [f"? (cannot compare: {e})"]
    signature = signature_state()
    print(f"hash: {full}")
    print(f"subject: {subj}")
    print(f"signature: {signature}")
    receipt = {
        "hash": full,
        "subject": subj,
        "branch": git(["rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"]).stdout.strip() or None,
        "committed_at": committed_at(),
        "paths": paths,
        "message_file": rel_msg,
        "co_author": co_author_note,
        "signature": signature,
        "tree": "changed by a hook" if hooked else "named paths not committed" if named_dirty else ("dirty" if dirty else "clean"),
        "dirty_paths": named_dirty or dirty,
        "hook_changed": hooked,
        "gate": gate_record,
        "exit": 1 if hooked or named_dirty or (dirty and not allow_dirty) else 0,
    }
    try:
        print(f"receipt: {write_receipt(root, receipt)}")
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"receipt: not written ({e}); the commit {full[:12]} exists, its receipt is missing", file=sys.stderr)
    if hooked or named_dirty:
        if hooked:
            print(f"ctlhq-commit: the commit {full[:12]} exists, but it differs from the index ctlhq-commit checked just before git commit:", file=sys.stderr)
            for l in hooked:
                print(f"  {l}", file=sys.stderr)
        if named_dirty:
            print(f"ctlhq-commit: the commit {full[:12]} exists, but {len(named_dirty)} named path{'s are' if len(named_dirty) != 1 else ' is'} not committed:", file=sys.stderr)
            for l in named_dirty:
                print(f"  {l}", file=sys.stderr)
        print("why: a hook or another process changed the index or the files during git commit, so no gate checked what was committed; --allow-dirty never accepts this.", file=sys.stderr)
        parent = git(["rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", "HEAD^"]).stdout.strip() or None
        if parent == head_before:
            undo_cmd = "git reset --soft HEAD~1" if head_before else "git update-ref -d HEAD"
            undo_text = f"Check it with `git show --stat {full[:12]}` and undo it with `{undo_cmd}` if it is wrong"
        elif head_before:
            b = head_before[:12]
            undo_text = (f"HEAD's parent is not {b}, the commit HEAD named before this run, so another commit was made too (a hook "
                         f"can do this). Check `git log {b}..HEAD`, and go back with `git reset --soft {b}` only if every commit after {b} is wrong")
        else:
            undo_text = ("This run made the first commit, but HEAD has a parent, so another commit was made too (a hook can do this). "
                         "Check `git log` before you undo anything")
        print(f"fix: the commit {full[:12]} exists. {undo_text}; then check the repository's hooks and run ctlhq-commit again.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if dirty:
        tail = "accepted by --allow-dirty" if allow_dirty else "not clean"
        print(f"tree: dirty ({len(dirty)} path{'s' if len(dirty) != 1 else ''}), {tail}")
        for l in dirty:
            print(f"  {l}")
        if not allow_dirty:
            print(f"ctlhq-commit: the commit {full[:12]} exists, but the tree is dirty afterwards; commit or restore those paths, or pass --allow-dirty", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
    else:
        print("tree: clean")
    print(f"committed {full}")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
